// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Human geography asks why things are where they are, and why it matters. Maps are our main tool.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Every map projection distorts something: shape, area, distance or direction. The Mercator projection preserves direction (useful for navigation) but greatly exaggerates areas near the poles. Equal-area projections like Gall-Peters preserve area but distort shape."},{"t":"key","text":"Key spatial concepts: location (absolute and relative), place, space, distance decay (interaction decreases with distance), time-space compression (technology shrinks effective distance), pattern and diffusion."},{"t":"p","text":"Thematic maps show data: choropleth maps use shading by region, dot density maps show distributions, graduated symbol maps use sized symbols, and isoline maps connect equal values."},{"t":"p","text":"Geographic data comes from field work, census data, GPS, remote sensing (satellites) and GIS (layering digital data)."}]},{"say":"Patterns look different at different scales. Geographers also divide the world into regions to study it.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Scale of analysis matters: global, national, regional and local scales can reveal different patterns. A country can look wealthy nationally while some local areas are very poor."},{"t":"p","text":"Types of regions:"},{"t":"list","items":["Formal (uniform) regions share a common characteristic (a country, a language area).","Functional (nodal) regions are organized around a central point (a newspaper's delivery area, a metro area).","Perceptual (vernacular) regions are based on people's feelings and ideas (the \"South\" or \"Midwest\")."]},{"t":"p","text":"Human–environment interaction: environmental determinism claims the environment shapes culture; possibilism argues people make choices within environmental limits."}]}],[{"say":"Where people live, and how fast populations grow, shapes everything from economies to politics.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Population density: arithmetic density (people per land area), physiological density (people per arable land, showing pressure on farmland), and agricultural density (farmers per arable land)."},{"t":"key","text":"The Demographic Transition Model: Stage 1 (high birth and death rates), Stage 2 (death rates drop, rapid growth), Stage 3 (birth rates fall), Stage 4 (low rates, stable), Stage 5 (possible decline)."},{"t":"p","text":"Population pyramids show age and sex structure: wide bases mean rapid growth; narrow bases mean slow growth or decline."},{"t":"p","text":"Theories: Malthus predicted population would outgrow food supply; critics (Boserup) argue technology increases food production. Policies can be pronatalist (encouraging births, like in aging countries) or antinatalist (like China's former one-child policy)."}]},{"say":"People move for many reasons. Migration reshapes both the places people leave and the places they go.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Push factors drive people away (war, poverty, persecution, disasters); pull factors attract them (jobs, safety, family). Intervening obstacles and opportunities affect the journey."},{"t":"p","text":"Types of migration: voluntary vs. forced (refugees fleeing persecution, internally displaced persons within their own country), internal vs. international, chain migration (following relatives) and step migration (moving in stages)."},{"t":"p","text":"Ravenstein's laws of migration: most migrants travel short distances; each migration flow produces a counterflow; long-distance migrants tend to go to big cities."},{"t":"p","text":"Remittances (money sent home by migrants) are a major source of income for many developing countries."}]}],[{"say":"Culture spreads across space through diffusion. Understanding how it spreads explains our cultural landscape.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Types of diffusion:"},{"t":"list","items":["Relocation diffusion: people move and bring their culture with them.","Expansion diffusion: culture spreads while staying strong at its origin. Hierarchical (from important people or places down), contagious (person to person, like a virus) and stimulus (an idea spreads but changes, like vegetarian burgers at fast-food chains in India)."]},{"t":"p","text":"Folk culture is traditional and local; popular culture is widespread and changes quickly, often spreading through media and globalization."},{"t":"p","text":"Cultural landscapes show human imprints on the land (buildings, farms, sacred sites). Acculturation, assimilation, syncretism (blending cultures) and multiculturalism describe cultural interaction."}]},{"say":"Language and religion are two of the most powerful parts of cultural identity.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Languages are grouped into families (Indo-European is the largest by speakers), branches and groups. A lingua franca is a common language used between speakers of different languages (English today)."},{"t":"key","text":"Universalizing religions seek converts worldwide and spread widely (Christianity, Islam, Buddhism). Ethnic religions are tied to a particular group and place and spread less (Hinduism, Judaism)."},{"t":"p","text":"Hearths are the places where cultures and religions originated. Christianity and Islam spread through relocation and expansion diffusion; Buddhism spread along trade routes through Asia."},{"t":"p","text":"Language and religion can unify people or divide them, shaping conflicts and political boundaries. Toponyms (place names) reveal history and culture."}]}],[{"say":"The political map divides the world into states. But states, nations and boundaries don't always line up.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"A state is a political unit with sovereignty over territory. A nation is a group with shared culture and identity. A nation-state has a single dominant nation (Japan); a multinational state contains several nations; a stateless nation lacks its own state (Kurds, Palestinians)."},{"t":"p","text":"Boundary types: geometric (straight lines, often drawn by colonizers), physical (rivers, mountains) and cultural (language or religion). Antecedent, subsequent, superimposed and relic boundaries describe when they formed."},{"t":"p","text":"Colonial powers often drew superimposed boundaries in Africa that ignored ethnic groups, contributing to later conflicts."},{"t":"p","text":"Gerrymandering draws electoral districts for political advantage."}]},{"say":"Political power can shift down to regions or up to international organizations. Both are reshaping the world.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Devolution transfers power from a central government to regions, often driven by ethnic, linguistic, economic or geographic differences (Scotland in the UK, Catalonia in Spain)."},{"t":"p","text":"Centrifugal forces divide a state (ethnic conflict, inequality); centripetal forces unify it (national identity, shared institutions, infrastructure)."},{"t":"p","text":"Supranationalism: states join organizations that share some power, like the European Union (shared currency and open borders), the United Nations, NATO and the African Union."},{"t":"p","text":"Federal states share power with regional governments (US, Germany); unitary states concentrate power in the central government (France, Japan)."}]}],[{"say":"Agriculture transformed humanity, and it keeps changing how we use land.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The First Agricultural Revolution (Neolithic) domesticated plants and animals. The Second (British) Agricultural Revolution increased productivity with new tools and crop rotation. The Third (Green Revolution) used high-yield seeds, fertilizers and pesticides."},{"t":"p","text":"Intensive agriculture uses lots of labor or capital on small land areas (market gardening, plantations, rice farming). Extensive agriculture uses large land areas with fewer inputs (ranching, shifting cultivation, nomadic herding)."},{"t":"p","text":"Subsistence farming feeds the farmer's family; commercial farming sells for profit. Agribusiness and commodity chains connect farms to global markets."},{"t":"p","text":"The Columbian Exchange spread crops and livestock between hemispheres."}]},{"say":"Why are some crops grown near cities and others far away? Von Thünen's model explains it.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Von Thünen's model: around a central market, rings of land use form based on transportation costs. Perishable, heavy goods (dairy, vegetables) are produced nearest; then forestry (heavy wood); then grains; then ranching farthest away."},{"t":"p","text":"Modern refrigeration and transportation have changed the pattern, but the logic of bid-rent and transport costs still applies."},{"t":"p","text":"Agricultural challenges: desertification, soil salinization, deforestation for farmland, water use and food deserts (areas with poor access to healthy food). Sustainable practices include conservation tillage, crop rotation, organic farming and urban farming."},{"t":"p","text":"Women play a major role in agriculture worldwide, especially in subsistence farming."}]}],[{"say":"Most people now live in cities. Geographers study why cities grow where they do and how they're organized.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Urbanization is the growth of cities as people move from rural areas. Megacities have over 10 million people; world cities (global cities) like New York, London and Tokyo are hubs of finance and culture."},{"t":"key","text":"The rank-size rule says the nth largest city is 1/n the size of the largest. A primate city is much larger than the next city and dominates the country (Paris, Bangkok)."},{"t":"p","text":"Central place theory (Christaller): cities provide goods and services to surrounding areas. Threshold is the minimum population needed to support a service; range is the maximum distance people will travel for it."},{"t":"p","text":"Suburbanization grew with cars and highways, leading to urban sprawl, edge cities and boomburbs."}]},{"say":"City models show patterns in how land is used. Real cities also face challenges like inequality and sprawl.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Classic models: Concentric Zone (Burgess; rings around the CBD), Sector (Hoyt; wedges along transportation routes), Multiple Nuclei (Harris and Ullman; several centers). Other models describe Latin American, African and Southeast Asian cities."},{"t":"p","text":"Bid-rent theory: land closest to the central business district (CBD) is most expensive, so it's used most intensively (skyscrapers)."},{"t":"p","text":"Urban challenges:"},{"t":"list","items":["Redlining and segregation, gentrification (wealthier residents moving in and displacing lower-income residents), and informal settlements (squatter settlements).","Sprawl increases car dependence and pollution."]},{"t":"p","text":"Solutions: smart growth, new urbanism (walkable, mixed-use), public transit and greenbelts."}]}],[{"say":"Some countries are much wealthier than others. Geographers measure and explain these differences.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Measures of development: GDP and GNI per capita (economic output per person), the Human Development Index (HDI combines income, education and life expectancy), and the Gender Inequality Index."},{"t":"p","text":"The economy has sectors: primary (extracting resources, farming), secondary (manufacturing), tertiary (services), quaternary (information and research) and quinary (top-level decision making). Developed countries have more workers in tertiary and higher sectors."},{"t":"p","text":"Women's empowerment, education and access to microloans help development."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Country A has a high GDP per capita but low life expectancy and literacy. What does its HDI likely show?","steps":["HDI weighs health and education along with income"],"answer":"A lower HDI than its income alone suggests, revealing uneven development"}]},{"say":"Why do some places develop faster? Different theories offer different answers.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Rostow's stages of growth: traditional society → preconditions for takeoff → takeoff → drive to maturity → high mass consumption. Critics say it assumes all countries follow the Western path."},{"t":"p","text":"Wallerstein's world systems theory divides countries into core (wealthy, high-skill), semi-periphery and periphery (poorer, providing raw materials and cheap labor), emphasizing unequal global relationships."},{"t":"p","text":"Globalization connects economies through trade, outsourcing and multinational corporations. Special economic zones and export processing zones attract investment. The new international division of labor moves manufacturing to lower-wage countries."},{"t":"p","text":"Sustainable development aims to meet present needs without harming future generations, as in the UN Sustainable Development Goals."}]}]];
export default text;
